1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,761
Hello!

2
00:00:00,761 --> 00:00:03,066
This week has two focus areas.

3
00:00:03,066 --> 00:00:08,906
In Listening, you will practise writing names, numbers, and times correctly,

4
00:00:08,906 --> 00:00:11,211
and avoiding correction traps.

5
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:17,820
In Speaking Part 1, you will learn to answer in two to three sentences using the A R E

6
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:18,588
framework.

7
00:00:19,888 --> 00:00:26,143
Section 1 is an everyday conversation between two people, and you often fill in a form.

8
00:00:26,143 --> 00:00:30,314
Section 2 is one person talking about a place or an event,

9
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:33,693
often with multiple-choice questions and a map.

10
00:00:33,693 --> 00:00:37,360
In the real exam, you hear the recording only once.

11
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:41,761
Names and numbers must be exactly right.

12
00:00:41,761 --> 00:00:47,111
Easily confused pairs are thirteen and thirty, and fifteen and fifty.

13
00:00:47,111 --> 00:00:48,739
Listen to the stress.

14
00:00:48,739 --> 00:00:52,383
Numbers ending in teen are stressed at the end,

15
00:00:52,383 --> 00:00:56,492
while numbers ending in ty are stressed at the start.

16
00:00:57,792 --> 00:00:59,707
Thirteen.

17
00:00:59,707 --> 00:01:01,197
Thirty.

18
00:01:01,197 --> 00:01:02,899
Fifteen.

19
00:01:02,899 --> 00:01:04,176
Fifty.

20
00:01:07,476 --> 00:01:10,102
Her surname is Harrington.

21
00:01:10,102 --> 00:01:12,828
That's H-A-R-R-I-N-G-T-O-N.

22
00:01:16,128 --> 00:01:21,485
Oh seven seven oh oh, nine oh oh, two seven three.

23
00:01:21,485 --> 00:01:23,092
Half past nine.

24
00:01:23,092 --> 00:01:25,128
A quarter to eight.

25
00:01:28,428 --> 00:01:34,276
Before you listen, look at the words around the gap to predict the type of word you need.

26
00:01:34,276 --> 00:01:38,547
After a pound sign comes a number, after a comes a singular noun,

27
00:01:38,547 --> 00:01:40,715
and after must comes a base verb.

28
00:01:40,715 --> 00:01:45,972
Also underline the word limit, because writing more words than allowed is wrong.

29
00:01:47,272 --> 00:01:48,618
Three common traps.

30
00:01:48,618 --> 00:01:50,034
The correction trap:

31
00:01:50,034 --> 00:01:54,568
the speaker changes their mind, so choose the final information.

32
00:01:54,568 --> 00:01:55,913
The rejection trap:

33
00:01:55,913 --> 00:02:00,517
if they say that date is fully booked, then it is not the answer.

34
00:02:00,517 --> 00:02:02,359
The used to, but now trap:

35
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:07,600
when you hear used to followed by but now, choose the current information.

36
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,736
The class meets on Mondays.

37
00:02:11,736 --> 00:02:14,467
Oh, sorry, I mean Fridays.

38
00:02:14,467 --> 00:02:16,988
Which day is the answer?

39
00:02:21,288 --> 00:02:23,255
Now to Speaking Part 1.

40
00:02:23,255 --> 00:02:29,157
Each answer is two to three sentences, following the A R E framework.

41
00:02:29,157 --> 00:02:30,269
A for answer:

42
00:02:30,269 --> 00:02:31,552
reply directly.

43
00:02:31,552 --> 00:02:32,663
R for reason:

44
00:02:32,663 --> 00:02:33,861
give a reason.

45
00:02:33,861 --> 00:02:34,887
E for extra:

46
00:02:34,887 --> 00:02:37,539
add an example or an exception.

47
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:43,440
Don't give one-word answers, and don't memorise a long speech either.

48
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:46,727
Do you enjoy reading?

49
00:02:46,727 --> 00:02:48,336
Yes, quite a lot.

50
00:02:48,336 --> 00:02:52,594
I read before bed, because it helps me relax.

51
00:02:52,594 --> 00:02:56,284
Lately I've been reading short stories.

52
00:02:59,584 --> 00:03:01,879
Did you play sports as a child?

53
00:03:01,879 --> 00:03:07,432
I used to play table tennis after school, but these days I prefer swimming.

54
00:03:10,732 --> 00:03:13,252
Two common mistakes in Part 1.

55
00:03:13,252 --> 00:03:13,588
One:

56
00:03:13,588 --> 00:03:17,200
answering just yes or no and then stopping.

57
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:17,536
Two:

58
00:03:17,536 --> 00:03:22,576
memorising a long speech, so you sound like you are reading.

59
00:03:22,576 --> 00:03:25,096
Use your own real information.

60
00:03:25,096 --> 00:03:28,540
Now, let's do a three-question mini quiz.

61
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:31,226
Question one.

62
00:03:31,226 --> 00:03:33,999
Meet at a quarter to nine.

63
00:03:33,999 --> 00:03:35,705
What time is it?

64
00:03:35,705 --> 00:03:37,091
Question two.

65
00:03:37,091 --> 00:03:39,757
The trip is on the tenth.

66
00:03:39,757 --> 00:03:43,062
Oh, sorry, I mean the eleventh.

67
00:03:43,062 --> 00:03:45,728
Which date is the answer?

68
00:03:51,028 --> 00:03:52,468
Question three.

69
00:03:52,468 --> 00:03:54,676
Which answer is better?

70
00:03:54,676 --> 00:03:56,692
Do you like your job?

71
00:03:56,692 --> 00:03:56,884
A:

72
00:03:56,884 --> 00:03:57,268
Yes.

73
00:03:57,268 --> 00:03:57,460
B:

74
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:04,852
Yes, because my colleagues are friendly and I learn something new every week.

75
00:04:10,152 --> 00:04:10,804
Answers.

76
00:04:10,804 --> 00:04:11,130
One:

77
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:17,488
eight forty-five, because a quarter to nine means fifteen minutes before nine.

78
00:04:17,488 --> 00:04:17,814
Two:

79
00:04:17,814 --> 00:04:22,624
the eleventh, because the speaker corrects the information.

80
00:04:22,624 --> 00:04:23,113
Three:

81
00:04:23,113 --> 00:04:28,248
B, because it has a direct answer, a reason, and an extra idea.

82
00:04:30,548 --> 00:04:31,117
Summary:

83
00:04:31,117 --> 00:04:36,381
when you listen, write exactly what you hear, predict the word type first,

84
00:04:36,381 --> 00:04:38,871
and wait for the final information.

85
00:04:38,871 --> 00:04:43,922
When you speak in Part 1, answer with A R E, in two to three sentences,

86
00:04:43,922 --> 00:04:45,629
using the correct tense.

87
00:04:45,629 --> 00:04:51,035
Do the exercises in the app, record yourself answering ten Part 1 questions,

88
00:04:51,035 --> 00:04:53,596
and compare them with the checklist.

89
00:04:53,596 --> 00:04:58,220
The weekly test has a Listening target of at least sixty percent.
